  "prompt": "TASK: context-free grammar (in Chomsky normal form) for a constrained bracket language\n\nAlphabet: the four symbols ( ) [ ] (round and square brackets).\nA word is WELL-NESTED if its symbols can be paired so that every ( is paired with a later ), every [ with a later ], and pairs are properly nested (no crossing, as in ([)]). A paired opening and closing symbol together with everything between them is a GROUP; it is a (-group or a [-group according to its opening symbol. The DEPTH of a group is 1 plus the number of groups that strictly contain it, so top-level groups have depth 1. The PARENT of a group is the smallest group that strictly contains it; top-level groups have no parent.\n\nL is the set of words w such that:\n  (1) w is non-empty and well-nested;\n  (2) no group has depth greater than 3;\n  (3) no (-group has a [-group as its parent.\n(-groups may appear at top level or with a (-group as parent. [-groups may have any parent, subject to (2).\n\nExamples in L: (), [](), ([]), (([])), [[[]]]\nExamples not in L: ε (empty), (] (not well-nested), [()] (a (-group whose parent is a [-group), [[()]] (same reason), (((()))) (depth 4)\n\nGive a grammar in Chomsky normal form that generates exactly L.\n\nWHAT TO SUBMIT\nA context-free grammar in Chomsky normal form (CNF), as one JSON object with these keys:\n  \"start\"       : the start nonterminal\n  \"productions\" : list of objects {\"lhs\": <nonterminal>, \"rhs\": [<symbol>, ...]}, one object per alternative\nSymbols: an rhs entry that is exactly one of the terminal symbols ( ) [ ] is a terminal; every other name is a nonterminal. Use names without spaces; uppercase names such as S, A1, X_2 are recommended.\nCNF means every production has exactly one of these two forms:\n  A -> B C   (rhs is a list of two nonterminals), or\n  A -> t     (rhs is a list containing exactly one terminal).\nNot allowed: ε-productions (empty rhs), unit productions A -> B, right-hand sides with three or more symbols, and mixed right-hand sides such as A -> t B. The start symbol may also appear on right-hand sides. A CNF grammar cannot generate ε, which is why L excludes ε.\n\nHOW IT IS GRADED\n1. The grammar must be in CNF exactly as defined above (otherwise score 0).\n2. For EVERY word w over the alphabet with 1 <= |w| <= 16: the grammar must generate w if and only if w is in L. This is checked exhaustively by an exact algorithm, not by sampling.\n3. The grammar must generate infinitely many words, because L is infinite.\nScore: 1.0 if all three checks pass, otherwise 0. Equivalence of context-free grammars is undecidable in general, so the check is exact only for words up to length 16; your grammar should nevertheless generate exactly L.\n\nFORMAT EXAMPLE (for an unrelated language { x^n y^n : n >= 1 } over {x, y})\n```json\n{\"start\": \"S\", \"productions\": [\n  {\"lhs\": \"S\", \"rhs\": [\"X\", \"Y\"]}, {\"lhs\": \"S\", \"rhs\": [\"X\", \"T\"]},\n  {\"lhs\": \"T\", \"rhs\": [\"S\", \"Y\"]},\n  {\"lhs\": \"X\", \"rhs\": [\"x\"]}, {\"lhs\": \"Y\", \"rhs\": [\"y\"]}]}\n```",
